# artist key: name, years, tradition, bio, source(title,url), photo key or None
A={
'handel':('George Frideric Handel','1685–1759','Classical','German-born composer who made his career in London. He wrote *Messiah* in about 24 days in 1741 to a libretto by Charles Jennens drawn entirely from Scripture; it premiered in Dublin on 13 April 1742 as a charity concert for prisoners and hospitals. Handel conducted it yearly for London\'s Foundling Hospital.',('Library of Congress: Handel\'s Messiah','https://www.loc.gov/audio/?q=handel+messiah')),
'bach':('Johann Sebastian Bach','1685–1750','Classical','Lutheran church musician, cantor of St Thomas, Leipzig, from 1723. He wrote some 200 surviving church cantatas for the Sundays of the year, the *St John* (1724) and *St Matthew* (1727) Passions for Good Friday, and the *Christmas Oratorio* (1734). He signed many scores "S.D.G.", *Soli Deo Gloria*, to God alone the glory.',('Bach-Archiv Leipzig','https://www.bach-leipzig.de/en')),
'mozart':('Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart','1756–1791','Classical','Austrian composer, raised in the Catholic cathedral music of Salzburg. His *Requiem* in D minor was unfinished at his death in December 1791 and completed by his pupil Franz Xaver Süssmayr. He also wrote the *Coronation Mass* and *Ave verum corpus* (1791) for Corpus Christi.',('Internet Archive: Mozart Requiem',ia('lp_requiem_volfgang-amadeus-mozart-karl-bhm-viener-ph'))),
'faure':('Gabriel Fauré','1845–1924','Classical','French organist at the Madeleine in Paris and later director of the Paris Conservatoire. His gentle *Requiem* (1887–1890) leaves out the terrifying *Dies irae* and ends *In paradisum*, the prayer that angels lead the soul into paradise. He called it a lullaby of death.',('Internet Archive: Fauré Requiem',ia('faure_requiem'))),
'fisk':('Fisk Jubilee Singers','founded 1871','Spirituals','A choir of students, most of them formerly enslaved, from Fisk University in Nashville. They toured the US and Europe from 1871 to save their school from bankruptcy, sang for Queen Victoria, and brought spirituals like *Swing Low, Sweet Chariot* to concert halls for the first time. Their earnings built Jubilee Hall.',('Library of Congress: Fisk Jubilee Singers',loc('fisk jubilee singers'))),
'anderson':('Marian Anderson','1897–1993','Spirituals','Contralto from Philadelphia. Barred from Constitution Hall in 1939, she sang on Easter Sunday at the Lincoln Memorial for 75,000 people, opening with *My Country, \'Tis of Thee* and closing with spirituals. In 1955 she became the first Black soloist at the Metropolitan Opera.',('Library of Congress: Marian Anderson',loc('marian anderson'))),
'bwj':('Blind Willie Johnson','1897–1945','Holy blues','Texas street evangelist with slide guitar and a rasping bass voice, who recorded 30 sides for Columbia from 1927 to 1930, all religious. His wordless *Dark Was the Night, Cold Was the Ground*, a meditation on the Crucifixion, was sent into space on the Voyager Golden Record in 1977.',('Internet Archive: Blind Willie Johnson',ia('DarkWasTheNightColdWasTheGround-BlindWillieJohnson'))),
'davis':('Rev. Gary Davis','1896–1972','Holy blues','South Carolina-born guitarist and ordained Baptist minister who preached and sang on the streets of Durham and later Harlem. A master of ragtime-style fingerpicking, he refused for years to play "devil\'s music" and sang gospel blues: *Death Don\'t Have No Mercy*, *Samson and Delilah*, *I Am the Light of This World*.',('Library of Congress: Rev. Gary Davis',loc('reverend gary davis'))),
'house':('Son House','1902–1988','Holy blues','Mississippi Delta bluesman who was a preacher before he was a blues singer, and spent his life torn between the two. His *John the Revelator* (1965) sings the Book of Revelation over handclaps alone, and his *Preachin\' the Blues* is a sermon about a preacher who could not stop singing the blues.',('Library of Congress: Son House',loc('son house'))),
'dorsey':('Thomas A. Dorsey','1899–1993','Gospel','"The father of gospel music." As "Georgia Tom" he was a blues pianist for Ma Rainey. After a religious conversion he joined blues feeling to church song at Pilgrim Baptist Church in Chicago. When his wife Nettie died in childbirth in 1932, and their baby son soon after, he wrote *Take My Hand, Precious Lord*, later sung by Mahalia Jackson at Martin Luther King Jr.\'s funeral.',('Library of Congress: Thomas A. Dorsey',loc('thomas a dorsey'))),
'tharpe':('Sister Rosetta Tharpe','1915–1973','Gospel','Arkansas-born singer and electric guitarist who grew up in the Church of God in Christ and took gospel into nightclubs and the pop charts with *Rock Me* and *This Train* (1938). Her guitar playing on *Strange Things Happening Every Day* (1944) is often called the first rock and roll. She was inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame in 2018.',('Library of Congress: Sister Rosetta Tharpe',loc('rosetta tharpe'))),
'mahalia':('Mahalia Jackson','1911–1972','Gospel','New Orleans-born contralto, "the Queen of Gospel", who worked with Thomas A. Dorsey in Chicago. Her *Move On Up a Little Higher* (1947) sold millions. She refused to sing secular music, sang before the "I Have a Dream" speech at the March on Washington in 1963, and is said to have called out to King, "Tell them about the dream, Martin."',('Library of Congress: Mahalia Jackson',loc('mahalia jackson'))),
'dixie':('The Dixie Hummingbirds','founded 1928','Gospel quartet','Founded by James B. Davis in Greenville, South Carolina, with Ira Tucker as lead singer from 1938. Among the most influential of the "hard gospel" quartets, they won a Grammy for *Loves Me Like a Rock* with Paul Simon (1973) and sang for over 75 years.',('Rock & Roll Hall of Fame: Vocal group history (Library of Congress search)',loc('dixie hummingbirds'))),
'soulstirrers':('The Soul Stirrers','founded 1926','Gospel quartet','Texas quartet led by R. H. Harris, who added a second lead and a fifth voice that changed the quartet sound. Sam Cooke sang lead from 1950 to 1957 (*Touch the Hem of His Garment*, *Jesus Gave Me Water*) before carrying gospel phrasing into soul music.',('Internet Archive: Soul Stirrers with Sam Cooke',ia('soul-stirrers-1950-56-soul-stirrers-thats-heaven-to-me-sam-cooke'))),
'aretha':('Aretha Franklin','1942–2018','Gospel / soul','Daughter of the Detroit preacher C. L. Franklin, raised singing in New Bethel Baptist Church. *Amazing Grace* (1972), recorded live in a Los Angeles church with Rev. James Cleveland, is the best-selling live gospel album of all time.',('Library of Congress: Aretha Franklin',loc('aretha franklin'))),
'staples':('The Staple Singers','from 1948','Gospel / soul','Family group from Chicago led by Roebuck "Pops" Staples, a Mississippi Delta guitarist, with his daughters Mavis, Cleotha and Yvonne and son Pervis. Their gospel (*Uncloudy Day*, 1956) became the soundtrack of the civil-rights movement; *I\'ll Take You There* topped the charts in 1972.',('Library of Congress: Staple Singers',loc('staple singers'))),
'sacredharp':('Sacred Harp singing','from 1844','Appalachian / shape-note','A tradition named for *The Sacred Harp*, a tunebook compiled by B. F. White and E. J. King in Georgia in 1844, using four shaped noteheads so anyone could sight-read. Singers sit in a hollow square facing each other and sing full-voiced, with no instruments and no audience. Hymns like *Idumea* and *Wondrous Love* came through it.',('Library of Congress: Sacred Harp',loc('sacred harp'))),
'carter':('The Carter Family','1927–1956','Early country gospel','A. P. Carter, his wife Sara and his sister-in-law Maybelle of Maces Spring, Virginia, recorded at the Bristol Sessions in 1927, often called the "big bang" of country music. A. P. collected songs across the mountains, and Maybelle\'s "Carter scratch" guitar became the model for country guitar. *Can the Circle Be Unbroken* (1935) reworks a 1907 hymn by Ada Habershon and Charles Gabriel.',('Library of Congress: Carter Family',loc('carter family'))),
'riddle':('Lesley Riddle','1905–1979','Blues','A Black guitarist from Kingsport, Tennessee, who travelled with A. P. Carter on song-collecting trips around 1928–1931, memorising tunes while Carter wrote down the words. He taught Maybelle Carter his finger-picking and slide style. The Carter Family\'s *Cannonball Blues* and *Hello Stranger* came through him: a meeting point of blues and Appalachian gospel.',('Library of Congress: Lesley Riddle',loc('lesley riddle'))),
'monroe':('Bill Monroe','1911–1996','Bluegrass','Kentucky mandolinist, "the father of bluegrass", who named the music after his band, the Blue Grass Boys. He learned from his uncle Pendleton Vandiver and from Arnold Shultz, a Black fiddler and guitarist; that blues feeling runs through his "high lonesome" sound. His gospel quartets (*Wicked Path of Sin*, *Precious Memories*) were a set piece of every show.',('Library of Congress: Bill Monroe',loc('bill monroe'))),
'stanley':('The Stanley Brothers','1946–1966','Bluegrass','Carter and Ralph Stanley of Dickenson County, Virginia, raised on the Primitive Baptist lined-out hymn singing of the mountains. *Angel Band*, *Rank Stranger* and *Gathering Flowers for the Master\'s Bouquet* are classics; Ralph\'s unaccompanied *O Death* (2000) brought the old style to a new generation.',('Library of Congress: Stanley Brothers',loc('stanley brothers'))),
'hank':('Hank Williams','1923–1953','Early country gospel','Alabama-born singer who learned music in a Baptist church and, as tradition has it, from a Black street musician, Rufus "Tee-Tot" Payne. He wrote *I Saw the Light* (1947) after a late-night drive home; it closed his shows for the rest of his short life. He also recorded sermon-songs as "Luke the Drifter".',('Library of Congress: Hank Williams',loc('hank williams'))),
'louvin':('The Louvin Brothers','1940–1963','Early country gospel','Ira and Charlie Loudermilk of Sand Mountain, Alabama, raised on Sacred Harp singing, sang some of the tightest close harmony in country music. Their gospel albums *Nearer My God to Thee* and *Satan Is Real* (1959) set Bible warnings to mountain harmony.',('Internet Archive: Louvin Brothers',ia('09.-the-drunkards-doom'))),
}
